A||||13||88/2.8||Hypertech Chip Speed Boost||||6||
Z||000000||88/2.8||02-13-2001||10:45 AM||||Is the hypertech chip really worth the money??? How much does it actually improve performance?????  <P>My Cars:<BR>88 V6 formula red/grey<BR>79 Formula Firebird 400 4 spd. black/white||||reg||6
Z||000001||Bruce||02-13-2001||11:03 AM||||No and none. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||02-13-2001||11:47 AM||||Wow, that's harsh.  First hand experience?  Anyone else care to input?  I had a Hypertech Stage II chip in my '87 Grand National and it made a HUGE difference in performace, but it did have the ability to play with the turbo boost.  I was planning on getting one for my 88 Formula, mainly to get the engine recalibrated to run with the 160 theromostat and low temp fan switch, but the performance increase would be nice.  ||||reg||9
Z||000003||87GT||02-13-2001||01:54 PM||||I had a hypertech chip in my 87GT 5-Sp which was put in by the previous owner.  I recently decided to take it out and put the stock chip back in just to see what the difference was.. The only difference it made was the gas mileage got better.  Performance was no different.  I think they are a waste of money.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Black88GT||02-13-2001||01:59 PM||||Not sure about other cars but chips for Fiero's are wastes of money.  Unless of course you have a modded engine where you need to get a custom chip made for the right fuel mixture.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||02-13-2001||02:34 PM||||Turbo engines are the only time substantial increases in performance can almost be guaranteed since you can just crank up the boost. On NA engine or supercharged engines, it far harder since GM does a good job of tuning the chips and the boost is limited by the pulley on the SC engines. <P>So it's no surprise that a GN chip was noticably better, but on something like the Fiero's, I'd only do it if there has been some major mechanical changes and only if the chip is made to match thoses changes. <P>IMHO..... ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Steven||02-13-2001||04:35 PM||||Would a stroking a engine to 3.1 liter,and then boring it to 3.2 liters and adding a new Crane Compucam 2030 be a good reason to change chips?||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||02-13-2001||04:50 PM||||Maybe, the value of a scanner can't be under-estimated here. The Fiero stock code can likely handle the extra displacement using stock injectors, but if you change the injectors or go much higher in displacement, it would certainly be a good idea. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Formula88||02-13-2001||11:38 PM||||Any thoughts on the chip solely as a cooling aid?  Use it with the 160 thermostat and low temp fan switch to retune the engine to run at the cooler temps?  Or would you just use the stat and switch?  I would think the car wouldn't run as well at the colder temp without the different chip. You could actually lose performance because the car would still think the engine is too cold.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Haze_Performance||02-14-2001||06:34 AM||||I had an ADS Super Chip in a 1990 Iroc & it made a huge differnce. It had more get up and go, ran cooler & improved my gas mileage.<P>I have one in my '87 SE now along with a 160 thermostat and low temp fan switch. But its not on the road yet. Ill have to do some testing.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.ws6fiero.com" TARGET=_blank><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ttttttt.gif">  </A>       <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/WS6_Fiero/photo.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>PFF Members Photo Page</A><BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1431213" TARGET=_blank><BR>Photopoint Gallery</A><p>[This message has been edited by Haze_Performance (edited 02-14-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000010||Tom Philips||02-14-2001||07:31 AM||||There is not a whole lot that they can change in the calibration to make the engine run any better the o2 sensor will adjust a/f ratio to 14.7 they could possibly give it more fuel when in power enrichment (wot) they could change spark cal but then you could get into knock .. Keep your stock cal and save your money||||reg||1
Z||000011||ThatRickGuy||02-14-2001||09:55 AM||||Just a note on the 3.2 vs chip. I did the 3.2 rebuild over fall and left the injectors, fuel presure and chip stock and have had no problems what so ever. plenty of power and everything. Personally I'm waiting on a chip until I put turbo on, but I may just go upto a 3.4 w/ turbo instead. But I agree with what most people are saying. The stock chip was put there by someone with a PHD, the new chip was made by 'some guy with a good idea'... Personally, I'll trust GM on this one.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Bruce||02-14-2001||03:06 PM||||Who sells the stock chip?||||reg||1
Z||000013||Formula88||02-15-2001||09:40 AM||||Tom, you're forgetting something.  The O2 sensor doesn't adjust the A/F ratio.  It talks to the ECM and the ECM controls the A/F ratio. A/F ratio, timing, and shift points on an automatic, are the main tuning changes used in a performance computer chip.  Altering the tuning to run at a cooler temp and possibly changing the rev limiter, etc. are done as well.  Whether or not any of this gives you more power is debatable.  I think there would be some improvement, but could anything short of a dyno see it?  Don't know.  My main question now is would it be worth it just to get the engine to run well, not necessarily better, at lower temps with the 160 thermo and low-temp fan switch.  I'm seriously considering this for the summer months.||||reg||6
